# Local Rally room state.
#
# ARP-R-06, 2026-08-04 — THE LEDGER IS NO LONGER DISTRIBUTED.
#
# `.rally/log/**`, `.rally/archive/**`, and `.rally/RETROSPECTIVE.md` used to be
# committed, on the reasoning that a fresh clone should see the project's
# coordination history. Measured, that reasoning did not survive contact with
# what the ledger actually contains:
#
#   * 3,680 facts replaying into 93 unreleased claims, 60 open handoffs
#     addressed to specific agent seats, 84 agent identities, and a foreign
#     lead seat. A cloner does not inherit history — they inherit the
#     maintainer's LIVE ROOM, complete with work assigned to machines they do
#     not have.
#   * 956 matches for `tyrones-macbook-pro-2.local` and `/Users/tyroneross/…`
#     across 25 tracked files, 544 of them in `.rally/`. Active claim scopes
#     name personal files (`Movies/rally-hero-video/…`,
#     `.claude/projects/…/memory/MEMORY.md`) and private sibling repos.
#   * 106 KB of peer prose per room read, on every read, forever.
#
# The ledger remains the canonical local record and is still append-only,
# replayable, and machine-portable — it is simply not something a stranger
# should receive by cloning. `.rally/manifest.json` still ships, so an agent
# landing in a fresh clone still finds the rally point; it just finds an EMPTY
# room, which is the correct starting state for someone else's repository.
#
# WHAT DE-TRACKING DOES NOT DO: the bytes remain in git history. A cloner can
# still recover every one of them with `git log`. Removing them for real means
# rewriting history, which is irreversible and is a separate, deliberate
# decision — see the release-hygiene note in docs/security/TRUST-MODEL.md.
#
# Everything else under .rally/ is a derived cache (facts.db, room.db,
# cursors.json, .reconcile-cache.json, tmp files) that `rally` rebuilds from
# the ledger on first open.
.rally/*
!.rally/manifest.json
.rally2/

# build-loop's own runtime + planning artifacts. build-loop is one optional
# tool that may be used to *build* agent-rally-point on Claude hosts; its
# files belong to itself, not to this repo. Ignore the whole directory.
.build-loop/

# uv lockfile — library-style project; downstream pipx installs resolve own deps.
uv.lock
# Exception: vendored watcher is an app-style daemon + port reference — ship its lock for reproducible installs.
!tools/agent-rally-watcher/uv.lock
.claude/worktrees/

# Optional tool runtime dirs (per-tool data lives under .<toolname>/, not in-repo).
.bookmark/
.navgator/
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
.claude/settings.local.json
.ibr/

# cockpitd runtime SQLite (created by `cockpitd serve` default COCKPIT_DB)
cockpitd.db
cockpitd.db-shm
cockpitd.db-wal
# The audit log is a sibling of COCKPIT_DB, so it lands here too.
cockpitd.db.audit.db
cockpitd.db.audit.db-shm
cockpitd.db.audit.db-wal

# Runtime coordination state + backups + local bundles (never commit; see 2026-07-02 takeover)
.rally.bak-*/
# archive/bundles/ was listed here from 2026-07-02 and tracked anyway: .gitignore has
# no effect on already-tracked paths, so the rule read as policy while 18 bundles
# (68.6 MiB) stayed in HEAD. De-tracked 2026-08-04 (ARP-R-06). The bundles carry full
# pre-sanitization history of this repo AND sibling repos; see the release-hygiene note
# in docs/security/TRUST-MODEL.md for what de-tracking does and does not remove.
# ARP-R-06: the whole of archive/ is de-tracked, not just bundles/. It also held
# `stashes-2026-06-11/` — six recovery patches plus a bundle, carrying 30
# `/Users/tyroneross/…` paths and the maintainer's hostname. Same class as the
# bundles: internal recovery artifacts that a cloner has no use for and that
# leak the machine they were cut on. Files stay on disk; history still has them.
archive/
agent-rally-point.worktrees/
RALLY-VERSION-MISMATCH-ASSESSMENT-*.md

# Vendored [patch] crates resolve through the workspace's root Cargo.lock, so a
# lock generated inside the vendored dir governs nothing and only churns.
vendor/*/Cargo.lock
